.TH gen_stack 3 "03/21/95" "CADC"
.SH NAME
gen_stack, stack_bottom, stack_down, stack_free, stack_init, stack_item, stack_pop, stack_position, stack_push, stack_top, stack_up \- operations on dynamic stacks.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.LP
.nf
.ft B
#include "gen_types.h"
#include "gen_stack.h"
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ta 0.8i
.ft B
pointer	stack_bottom( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_down( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
void	stack_free( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
STACK	\(**stack_init( n, func )
int	n;
pfi	func;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_item( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_pop( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
int	stack_position( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_push( stack, item )
STACK	\(**stack;
pointer	item;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_top( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;
.ft
.fi
.LP
.nf
.ft B
pointer	stack_up( stack )
STACK	\(**stack;

.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
This library is a set of routines to manage a stack of pointers.
To use this as a "normal" stack, use stack_init, stack_push,
stack_pop and stack_free.  Stack_item will return the item on the
top of the stack.  To navigate the stack, the concept of current
item is used.  The routines stack_bottom, stack_down, stack_up,
stack_top are used to change the current item.  If a pop or a push
is executed while the current item is not the top of the stack,
then
.B all
items above the current item in the stack are deleted.

.LP
.B stack_bottom
sets the current item to the bottom item on the the given stack 
.I stack
and returns a pointer to that item.
If the stack is empty, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.


.LP
.B stack_down
sets the current item one down from its current position in the stack
.I stack
and returns a pointer to that item.
If the stack is empty or the current item is already at the bottom, 
the current item remains unchanged and a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.

.LP
.B stack_free
frees all of the allocated memory referenced by 
.IR stack,
including the stack items. It calls the function supplied to 
.B stack_init
to delete the items in the stack.

.LP
.B stack_init(\|)
allocates and initializes a
.SM STACK 
structure with 
an initial value of 
.I n 
elements in its
.I array and
returns a pointer to the newly created STACK structure.  
If a memory allocation error occurs, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.
The function
.I func
will be used whenever items are deleted from the stack by a pop.
This function should have only one parameter - the pointer to the item
being deleted.

.LP
.B stack_item
returns a pointer to the current item in the stack
.I stack.
If the stack is empty, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.

.LP
.B stack_pop
deletes the current item from the stack 
.I stack
using the function supplied with the
.B stack_init
call.
The current item is set one down from its current position in the stack
and a pointer to it is returned.
If the stack is was empty or is empty after the deletion, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.
Note that if the current item is not the top item in the stack, then all
items above it in the stack are deleted as well.

.LP
.B stack_position
returns an interger flag indicating where the current item position
is located in the stack.
.SM STACK_POS_BOTTOM
indicates that the current item is the bottom item on the stack and
that there are items above it.
.SM STACK_POS_EMPTY 
indicates that there are no items on the stack.
.SM STACK_POS_MIDDLE 
indicates that the current item has items above it and below it in the stack.
.SM STACK_POS_SINGLE 
indicates that the current item is the only item in the stack.
.SM STACK_POS_TOP 
indicates that the current item is the top item on the stack and that there
are items below it.

.LP
.B stack_push
adds the pointer to a new item
.I item
to the stack
.I stack 
above the current item.  If the current item is not the top
item on the stack, then all items above the current item are deleted before
the new item is added.  The current item is then set to the new item and
a pointer is returned.
If a memory allocation error occurs, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.

.LP
.B stack_top
sets the current item to the top item in the stack
.I stack
and returns a pointer to that item.
If the stack is empty, a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.

.LP
.B stack_up
sets the current item up one from its current position in the stack
.I stack
and returns a pointer to that item.
If the stack is empty or the current item is already at the top,
the current item remains unchanged and a
.SM NULL
pointer is returned.

